<!-- ============ CHAPTER 1 ============ -->
<section class="chapter" id="ch1">
  <div class="chapter-inner">
    <header class="chapter-header reveal">
      <div class="chapter-number">01</div>
      <div class="chapter-tag">// SIDE A &middot; TRACK 1 //</div>
      <h2 class="chapter-title">The Internet Explodes</h2>
      <div class="chapter-deck">dial-up, AOL, chat rooms, email, and two grad students in a garage</div>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">By the later 1990s, the internet stopped feeling like a secret passage used mostly by universities, programmers, and hobbyists. It became a household sound: the scrape, chirp, and electronic howl of a <span class="term" data-term="dial-up modem">dial-up modem</span> connecting through a phone line.</p>

      <p>Dial-up was slow by modern standards. A picture could load one strip at a time. Someone picking up the house phone could knock you offline. But to millions of people, it felt like entering a hidden city.</p>

      <div class="photo r1 tape reveal" style="max-width:480px;">
        <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Windows 98 desktop computer">
        <div class="cap">a Windows 98 tower :: the box that hummed through dinner</div>
      </div>

      <p>In 1995, Pew Research Center found that 14% of U.S. adults had internet access, and most were using slow dial-up connections. By 1998, internet news use had grown, and <span class="term" data-term="AOL">AOL</span>'s membership reportedly jumped from 8 million to 16 million after AOL 4.0 and its mass-mailed CD-ROM campaigns. Those AOL discs arrived everywhere: in cereal boxes, in Sunday papers, taped to magazines, and stuffed in mailboxes. They became a meme before the word meme meant what it does now.</p>

      <p>AOL was not just an internet provider. It was a front door: email, instant messages, screen names, chat rooms, news, games, and the famous "You've got mail" alert. For many people, AOL <em>was</em> the internet.</p>

      <div class="photo-row reveal">
        <div class="photo r2 tape">
          <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="AOL channels screen">
          <div class="cap">AOL channels :: a town built of buttons</div>
        </div>
        <div class="photo r1 tape">
          <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="AOL connecting screen">
          <div class="cap">that running yellow man :: the patron saint of patience</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>chat rooms, AIM, and the strange new social life</h3>

      <p><span class="term" data-term="chat room">Chat rooms</span> were online spaces where strangers typed messages to one another in real time. For many young people, this was thrilling and strange: friendship, flirting, fandom, lying, roleplay, and danger all crowded into the same glowing box. AOL Instant Messenger (AIM) launched in 1997 and gave teenagers their first taste of "always available." A door-slam sound meant a buddy signed off. A creaky door meant one signed on. Away messages became poetry.</p>

      <div class="photo r3 tape reveal" style="max-width:420px;">
        <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="AOL Instant Messenger buddy list">
        <div class="cap">AIM :: the original notification anxiety machine</div>
      </div>

      <h3>two grad students, one search engine</h3>

      <p>Then, in 1998, two Stanford graduate students, Larry Page and Sergey Brin, founded <span class="term" data-term="Google">Google</span>. Their <span class="term" data-term="search engine">search engine</span> ranked pages partly by how other pages linked to them, which helped make searching the expanding Web more useful. Google was incorporated on September 4, 1998, in Menlo Park, California. At the time, almost nobody noticed.</p>

      <div class="photo r1 tape reveal" style="max-width:520px;">
        <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Larry Page and Sergey Brin">
        <div class="cap">Larry Page &amp; Sergey Brin :: the quiet end of the phone book</div>
      </div>

      <p>The late 1990s were the moment when information began to feel bottomless. Email replaced letters for many people. Websites replaced brochures. Search engines became maps. The Web was still awkward, slow, and full of dead links, but it carried a wild promise: somewhere out there, someone had already made a page about the thing you loved.</p>

      <div class="pull reveal">You did not "go online." You <em>connected</em>. The verb itself sounded like effort, like you were plugging your house into something larger than the house.</div>

      <div class="recap reveal">
        <h4>Quick Recap :: Track 1</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Pew (1995): ~14% of U.S. adults had internet access</li>
          <li>AOL doubled its membership from 8M to 16M around 1998</li>
          <li>Chat rooms and AIM transformed how young people socialized</li>
          <li>Google was incorporated September 4, 1998 in Menlo Park, CA</li>
          <li>The web was slow, but the promise was bottomless information</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="reflect reveal">
        <h5>What would you have done with a slow front door to the world?</h5>
        <p>Imagine you are 13 years old in 1998 and your family just got AOL. You have one phone line, one hour a night, and a modem that sings like a haunted teapot. What do you spend the hour on? Write a short answer below.</p>
