</section>

<!-- ============ CHAPTER 2 ============ -->
<section class="chapter" id="ch2">
  <div class="chapter-inner">
    <header class="chapter-header reveal">
      <div class="chapter-number">02</div>
      <div class="chapter-tag">// SIDE A &middot; TRACK 2 //</div>
      <h2 class="chapter-title">Machines in the<br>Living Room</h2>
      <div class="chapter-deck">N64, VHS, DVDs, cell phones, and one furry computer</div>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">The late 1990s were full of machines that felt half-familiar and half-magical. Cartridges clicked into slots like treasure. Discs spun in trays that opened like a butler's hand. Phones lived in pockets. A small plush thing on the shelf opened its eyes and said hello in a language it claimed to be inventing.</p>

      <h3>n64 :: the third dimension arrives</h3>

      <p>The <span class="term" data-term="Nintendo 64">Nintendo 64</span> arrived in North America in 1996 and helped define 3D gaming for a generation. Its controller had an analog stick, its cartridges clicked into place like treasure, and games such as <em>Super Mario 64</em>, <em>The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time</em>, <em>Mario Kart 64</em>, and <em>GoldenEye 007</em> made living rooms feel like portals. The console launched in Japan on June 23, 1996, and in North America on September 29, 1996.</p>

      <div class="photo r1 tape reveal" style="max-width:500px;">
        <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Super Mario 64 screenshot">
        <div class="cap">Super Mario 64 :: the day cameras learned to walk around</div>
      </div>

      <h3>vhs vs dvd :: the format war begins</h3>

      <p>At the same time, <span class="term" data-term="VHS">VHS</span> was still the king of home video. Families rented tapes from video stores, recorded TV shows, rewound cassettes, and stacked black plastic cases near the television. Be Kind, Rewind was an actual rule. Video stores were a kind of weekend ritual: walking the aisles, reading the backs of boxes, hoping the one you wanted was still on the shelf.</p>

      <p>But the next format had arrived. <span class="term" data-term="DVD">DVDs</span> stored video digitally on optical discs, could hold more data than CDs, and did not need rewinding. The format launched in the United States in 1997, and over time it began replacing VHS for movies and home entertainment. The picture was sharper. The case was smaller. The disc was strangely beautiful in the right light.</p>

      <div class="photo r2 tape reveal" style="max-width:440px;">
        <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Twister DVD case">
        <div class="cap">Twister on DVD :: rewinding is over forever</div>
      </div>

      <h3>cell phones :: the wall-mounted phone loses its grip</h3>

      <p>Early cell phones were also spreading, though they were not yet smartphones. They were mostly for calls, not apps, cameras, streaming, or social media. Many were bulky by today's standards, with antennas that pulled out like little flagpoles. But they changed expectations. People could be reached away from home, away from work, away from the wall-mounted phone in the kitchen.</p>

      <h3>furby :: the plush computer</h3>

      <p>Then came <span class="term" data-term="Furby">Furby</span>, released in 1998 by Tiger Electronics, a division of Hasbro. Furby seemed like a plush toy, but inside it were sensors, motors, and programmed responses. It "learned" from Furbish into English, reacted to touch and sound, and communicated with other Furbys by infrared. <em>Wired</em> described it in 1998 as an advanced <span class="term" data-term="animatronic">animatronic</span> toy with a 160-word English and "Furbish" lexicon.</p>

      <p>Furby felt cute and felt unsettling at the same time. It blinked. It giggled. It muttered in the dark if you forgot to take the batteries out. Rumors spread that Furbys could record conversations, which led the U.S. National Security Agency to ban them from its offices. The ban was based on a misunderstanding of how the toy worked, but the story said something true about the decade: people were beginning to wonder what their machines might be listening for.</p>

      <div class="photo r3 tape reveal" style="max-width:320px;">
        <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Furby toy">
        <div class="cap">Furby :: cute, sleepless, possibly plotting</div>
      </div>

      <div class="pull reveal">The future did not arrive all at once. It came as cartridges, discs, tapes, antennas, batteries, and toys that blinked back.</div>

      <div class="recap reveal">
        <h4>Quick Recap :: Track 2</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Nintendo 64 launched in NA on September 29, 1996</li>
          <li>VHS still dominated, but DVD arrived in the U.S. in 1997</li>
          <li>Cell phones spread but were not yet smartphones</li>
          <li>Furby (1998) used sensors, motors, and infrared to "talk" and learn</li>
          <li>Furbys were banned at NSA offices over (mistaken) recording fears</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="reflect reveal">
        <h5>Which late-90s machine would you have spent the most time with?</h5>
        <p>Pick one (N64, DVD player, a chunky cell phone, Furby) and explain in 3 to 5 sentences why. Try to argue more than just "it's fun." What did it actually change?</p>
        <textarea placeholder="your machine of choice..."></textarea>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ CHAPTER 3 ============ -->
<section class="chapter" id="ch3">
  <div class="chapter-inner">
    <header class="chapter-header reveal">
      <div class="chapter-number">03</div>
      <div class="chapter-tag">// SIDE A &middot; TRACK 3 //</div>
      <h2 class="chapter-title">The Sound<br>of Youth</h2>
      <div class="chapter-deck">grunge, alt rock, and a college kid named Napster</div>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">The late 1990s inherited the sound of the early decade: <span class="term" data-term="grunge">grunge</span> and <span class="term" data-term="alternative rock">alternative rock</span>. Grunge came especially from the <span class="term" data-term="Pacific Northwest">Pacific Northwest</span> and mixed punk's rawness with heavy guitar distortion and lyrics about alienation, depression, boredom, irony, and discomfort with commercial culture.</p>

      <p>Nirvana, Pearl Jam, Soundgarden, and Alice in Chains had broken through earlier in the decade, but their influence echoed across late-1990s rock. Alternative rock widened into many forms: college rock, post-grunge, industrial, Britpop, ska-punk, pop-punk, and heavier radio rock. The category that started as a small label for not-quite-mainstream guitar music became, by the end of the decade, basically the mainstream itself.</p>

      <div class="photo r1 tape reveal" style="max-width:520px;">
        <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Pearl Jam, Nirvana, Soundgarden, Alice in Chains">
        <div class="cap">the big four of grunge :: flannel, fuzz, and Seattle rain</div>
      </div>

      <p>This was not just music. It was a costume and a mood: flannel, thrift-store clothes, messy hair, basement bands, distortion pedals, and a suspicion that glossy success might be fake. Yet the contradiction was obvious. Anti-commercial music became commercially enormous. The machine learned to sell rebellion. Major labels mailed out flannel shirts as press kits.</p>

      <h3>napster :: songs without shelves</h3>

      <p>By the end of the decade, music was being changed by the internet. In June 1999, <span class="term" data-term="Napster">Napster</span> launched as a <span class="term" data-term="peer-to-peer">peer-to-peer</span> file-sharing service focused especially on <span class="term" data-term="MP3">MP3</span> music files. Users could search for songs and download them from other users' computers. It felt revolutionary because it made music seem instantly available, but it also created major copyright conflicts with artists and record labels. Napster was later shut down after lawsuits, but its brief rise helped preview the future of digital music.</p>

      <p>This was a turning point. In the old system, music moved through radio, record stores, MTV, CDs, and tapes. Napster suggested a different world: songs as files, libraries without shelves, sharing without permission, and listeners no longer waiting for the industry to decide what they could hear.</p>

      <div class="pull reveal">A song used to be a physical object with a sleeve and a tracklist. By 2000, for millions of people, a song was a small file with a filename that might or might not even be spelled right.</div>

      <p>The late 1990s soundscape was therefore split. One part was still physical: CDs, jewel cases, liner notes, burned mixes, Discman players. The other part was already becoming weightless, traveling through phone lines at midnight. Both parts existed in the same teenager's bedroom. The CD on the desk and the half-downloaded MP3 in the corner of the screen were two halves of the same future arguing with itself.</p>

      <div class="recap reveal">
        <h4>Quick Recap :: Track 3</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Grunge originated in the Pacific Northwest; key bands: Nirvana, Pearl Jam, Soundgarden, Alice in Chains</li>
          <li>Alternative rock expanded into Britpop, ska-punk, post-grunge, industrial, and more</li>
          <li>Anti-commercial music became hugely commercial: the central irony of the era</li>
          <li>Napster launched June 1999 as a peer-to-peer MP3 sharing service</li>
          <li>It was shut down by lawsuits, but it previewed the digital future of music</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="reflect reveal">
        <h5>Was Napster a revolution or a robbery?</h5>
