<!-- ============ CHAPTER 4 ============ -->
<section class="chapter" id="ch4">
  <div class="chapter-inner">
    <header class="chapter-header reveal">
      <div class="chapter-number">04</div>
      <div class="chapter-tag">// SIDE B &middot; TRACK 4 //</div>
      <h2 class="chapter-title">A World<br>Reorganizing</h2>
      <div class="chapter-deck">Europe binds itself together, a new kind of terror crosses borders, and a president nearly loses his job</div>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">While American teenagers clicked through chat rooms and played N64, the world map kept shifting. Some changes were planned, negotiated, and signed in long meetings. Others came in the form of explosions on television.</p>

      <h3>europe binds itself together</h3>

      <p>In Europe, integration moved forward. The <span class="term" data-term="Maastricht Treaty">Maastricht Treaty</span>, signed in 1992, entered into force on November 1, 1993, officially creating the <span class="term" data-term="European Union">European Union</span> and setting rules for deeper cooperation, including plans for a shared currency.</p>

      <div class="photo r1 tape reveal" style="max-width:520px;">
        <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Maastricht Treaty signing">
        <div class="cap">Maastricht, 1992 :: signatures that would echo for decades</div>
      </div>

      <p>Then, on January 1, 1999, the <span class="term" data-term="euro">euro</span> launched as an "invisible" currency used for accounting and electronic payments. Euro coins and banknotes would not arrive until 2002, but the monetary union had begun. For the first time in modern European history, countries that had spent the previous century repeatedly at war with each other were sharing a wallet.</p>

      <div class="photo-row reveal">
        <div class="photo r2 tape">
          <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="EU flag">
          <div class="cap">the EU flag :: twelve stars, one circle, no center</div>
        </div>
        <div class="photo r1 tape">
          <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="EU map">
          <div class="cap">the EU today :: a project that began as a treaty</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>terror crosses borders</h3>

      <p>At the same time, the rise of global <span class="term" data-term="terrorism">terrorism</span> became harder for Americans to ignore. In 1998, U.S. embassies in Nairobi, Kenya, and Dar es Salaam, Tanzania, were bombed. The U.S. later indicted Osama bin Laden and members of <span class="term" data-term="al-Qaeda">al-Qaeda</span> for the attacks. The State Department identifies the East Africa embassy bombings as a major moment in the U.S. confrontation with al-Qaeda.</p>

      <div class="photo r3 tape reveal" style="max-width:560px;">
        <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Nairobi embassy bombing aftermath">
        <div class="cap">Nairobi, August 7, 1998 :: a preview of a century not yet named</div>
      </div>

      <p>Most Americans, going about their lives, had never heard the name al-Qaeda. The name would not become familiar to most U.S. households for three more years. But by 1998, the network and the strategy that would define the early 2000s were already in motion. History was tightening even when it looked quiet.</p>

      <h3>the clinton scandal :: an impeachment, then an acquittal</h3>

      <p>At home, President Bill Clinton faced a major scandal. The issue involved Clinton's relationship with White House intern Monica Lewinsky and whether he lied under oath and obstructed justice. This should be handled plainly: it involved adult sexual misconduct, dishonesty, legal proceedings, and a fierce political battle.</p>

      <div class="photo-row reveal">
        <div class="photo r1 tape">
          <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Bill Clinton denying with Hillary Clinton">
          <div class="cap">"I did not have sexual relations with that woman."</div>
        </div>
        <div class="photo r2 tape">
          <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Bill Clinton and Monica Lewinsky">
          <div class="cap">the photo at the heart of a year of headlines</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>In December 1998, the House of Representatives <span class="term" data-term="impeachment">impeached</span> Clinton on two articles. In February 1999, the Senate acquitted him, so he remained president. The scandal mattered because it consumed national attention and deepened partisan conflict. Supporters often argued impeachment was excessive for the conduct involved. Critics argued that lying under oath and obstructing justice were serious abuses. Both views existed at the time, and historians still treat the event as a major episode in late-1990s political polarization.</p>

      <div class="pull reveal">The 1990s ended with the strange feeling that everything was connecting and also tearing along old seams. Europe was binding. Terror networks were globalizing. Washington was splitting along a fault line that would deepen for the next twenty-five years.</div>

      <div class="recap reveal">
        <h4>Quick Recap :: Track 4</h4>
        <ul>
