<!-- ============ CHAPTER 5 ============ -->
<section class="chapter" id="ch5">
  <div class="chapter-inner">
    <header class="chapter-header reveal">
      <div class="chapter-number">05</div>
      <div class="chapter-tag">// SIDE B &middot; TRACK 5 //</div>
      <h2 class="chapter-title">The Century's<br>Last Flicker</h2>
      <div class="chapter-deck">Columbine, Y2K, and a pineapple under the sea</div>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">The late 1990s had a bright cartoon surface, but a darker current ran underneath it. The decade was about to end. People felt it. Some prepared for celebration. Some prepared for collapse. Some did both at once.</p>

      <h3>columbine :: april 20, 1999</h3>

      <p>On April 20, 1999, two students attacked <span class="term" data-term="Columbine">Columbine High School</span> in Littleton, Colorado. They killed 12 students and one teacher, wounded many others, and then died by suicide. Britannica notes that although Columbine was neither the first nor the deadliest school shooting in U.S. history, it became a defining national event in debates over school violence, guns, bullying, media, security, and youth culture.</p>

      <div class="photo r1 tape reveal" style="max-width:440px;">
        <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Columbine students evacuating">
        <div class="cap">Columbine, April 20, 1999 :: a phrase that became shorthand for an entire problem</div>
      </div>

      <p>Columbine reshaped American schools. Lockdown drills, security cameras, "see something, say something" posters, and conversations about bullying, video games, music, and parenting all changed afterward. The event was discussed, argued over, mythologized, copied, condemned, and grieved for decades. The phrase "another Columbine" became its own piece of vocabulary.</p>

      <h3>y2k :: the clock that almost didn't work</h3>

      <p>That same year, the world braced for <span class="term" data-term="Y2K">Y2K</span>, also called the millennium bug. The problem came from older computer systems that stored years with only two digits. A system might read "00" as 1900 instead of 2000, creating possible errors in banking, utilities, transportation, records, and government systems. National Geographic explains Y2K as a computer flaw involving dates beyond December 31, 1999. Britannica notes that after major preparation and programming corrections, few major failures occurred when January 1, 2000 arrived.</p>

      <div class="photo r2 tape reveal" style="max-width:460px;">
        <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Y2K countdown clock">
        <div class="cap">December 31, 1999 :: the longest sixty seconds of the century</div>
      </div>

      <p>Y2K is sometimes remembered as a joke because the disaster did not happen. That memory is unfair. The disaster did not happen because thousands of programmers, governments, and businesses spent years quietly preventing it. It is one of the clearest cases in modern history where boring, expensive preparation worked exactly as intended. The world clicked over to 2000 with elevators still running and banks still open. The dullness was the victory.</p>

      <h3>spongebob :: a pineapple at the edge of the millennium</h3>

      <p>And then, somehow, from under the sea, came <span class="term" data-term="SpongeBob SquarePants">SpongeBob SquarePants</span>. Created by marine science educator and animator <span class="term" data-term="Stephen Hillenburg">Stephen Hillenburg</span>, <em>SpongeBob</em> first aired as a sneak peek on May 1, 1999, and officially premiered on Nickelodeon on July 17, 1999. It was absurd, loud, innocent, strange, and smarter than it looked.</p>

      <div class="photo r3 tape reveal" style="max-width:380px;">
        <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="SpongeBob SquarePants">
        <div class="cap">SpongeBob :: a yellow sponge laughing at the end of a century</div>
      </div>

      <p>It is strange to put SpongeBob in the same chapter as Columbine and Y2K. It is also fitting. The late 1990s held all of this at the same time: tragedy, technical anxiety, and a cartoon sponge flipping Krabby Patties for a generation that would grow up reciting his theme song. History does not stack neatly into categories. It happens in the same week, in the same living room, on the same channel.</p>

      <div class="pull reveal">The century ended not with silence, but with a dial-up tone: shrill, awkward, hopeful, and warning that everything was about to connect.</div>

      <p>So the later 1990s closed in contradiction. The Web was growing. Google had begun. Napster was loosening music from its plastic cases. DVDs were replacing VHS. Europe was binding itself together with a shared currency. Terror networks were moving across borders. American politics was becoming more bitter. Columbine shattered the myth that schools were automatically safe. Y2K made people wonder whether civilization had wired itself to a clock it barely understood. And then there was SpongeBob, flipping Krabby Patties in a pineapple under the sea, laughing at the edge of the millennium.</p>

      <div class="recap reveal">
        <h4>Quick Recap :: Track 5</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>April 20, 1999: Columbine killed 12 students + 1 teacher, reshaped school safety</li>
          <li>Y2K (millennium bug) involved 2-digit year storage misreading "00" as 1900</li>
          <li>Years of programming prevented Y2K disaster; the dullness was the victory</li>
          <li>SpongeBob premiered May/July 1999, created by Stephen Hillenburg (marine science educator)</li>
          <li>The decade ended in contradiction: tragedy and absurdity in the same week</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

